A||||15||FieroMike||I need HELP!!!! Ahhhhh!
Z||000000||FieroMike||07-25-99||03:37 PM||||Ok, thanks for your help in advance.  I dropped my engine and rebuilt and put it back and everything was fine.  The clutch went out about a week after, so I just dropped out the engine AGAIN and changed the clutch.  This time I disconnected the wiring from the ECM.  I put everything back to gether, opened the door, the dome light came on, turned the key forward, the air came on, dash lights, radio all that came on.  Turned the key and everything went out.  I could pull the two silver circuit breakers on the fuse box out and plug them back in and the dome light would come on, then turn the key and out it goes.  Now NOTHING comes on.  The battery IS new and charged, there are no pinched wires, no hot wires grounded that I can find.  Any suggestions!!! I need some help!!! Thanks!<p>Ogre: Any Ideas?<p>Thanks<br>Mike
Z||000001||batboy||07-25-99||04:45 PM||||If I understand this right, everything goes kaput when you try to crank the starter. I think that I'd double and triple check the starter solenoid wires. Something is shorting out, probably worse than just grounding out.
Z||000002||theogre||07-25-99||05:02 PM||||check the small wires from the battery. either of these damaged will kill the car.
Z||000003||Cooter||07-25-99||05:44 PM||||Make sure that you dont have any extra wires bolted on the starter with the battery cable. A car I bought had a set of ground wires bolted there and it really did some crazy stuff. 
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||07-25-99||07:03 PM||||Are the 2 bolts on the junction box tight?
Z||000005||Racer 1||07-25-99||09:04 PM||||You might have blown out some fusible links.Did you disconnect the battery when working on the engine the second time???<br>
Z||000006||FieroMike||07-25-99||09:32 PM||||Racer - No Battery in the car during the whole process!! Thank you guys I will try all of your suggestions and will write back!! Thanks a lot!
Z||000007||FieroMike||07-26-99||06:32 AM||||Well, I finally found out what was wrong.  It sounded like a power line was grounded, so I was looking in the wrong spot the whole time.  The small black wire that runs straight down off the negative battery cable was completely corroded.  A new wire and scraping the rust off the contact point proved to fix the whole problem.  Thank's a lot for the time and suggestions!!!  Thanks Ogre.  The starter was my main concern, but I guess I should have looked closer to the starting point! Thanks :)
Z||000008||theogre||07-26-99||08:23 AM||||For future ref,,,,,,,<p>The whole car is run off that anoying little red wire. This is a "feature" of most GM cars. The black wire can get you, like yours did, if the main ground strap from the block to the body is gone. So check the rest of your grounds, especially that braided one. Even if the braid one looks good you might want to replace it. they're cheap insurance. that cable should go from the block to the frame, not to the cradle.<p>It doesn't take much for it to screw up either. My + terminal is one of those replacement ones,,,,, It came loose just a tiny bit where it clamps onto the wire, and I got the same result you did.... turn the key, kill the car. As soon as I get a chance I'll have to replace the whole cable.
Z||000009||FieroMike||07-26-99||08:45 AM||||Ogre,<br>You know what's funny about that?  I was thinking that at the time, the big black ground is grounded to the engine, and the engine is grounded to the body, so why does it need two?  That braided line has been replaced by an extra piece of monster cable ground wire, what do you mean by cradle though?  Mine goes from the back right plenum bolt to the spring loaded hinge for the deck lid... is that right?  Thanks again for the help, I would change out the whole cable, I changed out the entire negative side, the positive looks ok.
Z||000010||Mark||07-27-99||08:56 PM||||The braided ground strap provides a current return path for the alternator to the car's electrical loads.  On mine, the nut that secures the strap to the hood hinge was slightly loose, resulting in a 2 to 3 volt drop from engine to chassis ground!
Z||000011||BENGAL4||07-28-99||07:32 AM||||If one end of the braided strap is connected to the deck lid, what is the other end connected to? Mine is just hanging from the deck lid.
Z||000012||theogre||07-28-99||07:45 AM||||the stock wire usually goes from the right deck hinge bracket on the car frame to the head or block.<p>As we've seen, the stupidest things screw around with these. Even if the cable looks good, you can still have poor connections because they are loose, painted, or dirty. It doesn't take much.
Z||000013||theogre||07-28-99||07:46 AM||||I ment the engine cradle..... some people forget it's not solid to the frame and try to ground things to it.
Z||000014||fred||07-30-99||04:34 PM||||fusible links or hot wire running from pos post
Z||000015||Racer 1||08-02-99||12:19 PM||||I just looked at my braided wire and it was broke in half.I just got a clutch job done I just wonder if the mechanic droped the engine while he had the clutch housing off and ripped the braided line.My car still ran fine when the line was broke but I fixed it just in case!!
